GTK made Qt
===========

Compile GTK 2, GTK 3 and GTK 4 programs - unmodified, as plain C - and have
them run on Qt 6 (or Qt 5.15).

Copyright (C) 2010-2014 Johan Thelin, Robin Burchell (gqt) and contributors.
Licensed under the LGPL 2.1 or later with a static linking exception; see
COPYING. The history of the two original projects is described in README.gqt.

    ./bgqt.sh gtk2/gcif          # a 2007 GTK 2 app, untouched, on Qt 6
    ./bgqt.sh gtk3/treeview
    ./bgqt.sh gtk4/application


How it works
------------

* The public headers (include/gtk/gtk.h, include/gdk/gdk.h) are plain C, so
  programs are compiled with gcc as they always were. No C++, no -fpermissive.

* Every widget is a real GObject (GtkWidget -> GtkContainer -> GtkBox ...),
  so g_signal_connect(), g_object_set(), properties, "notify::", refcounting,
  floating references, type checks and cast macros behave like GTK. Programs
  can subclass widgets with G_DEFINE_TYPE.

* Each GtkWidget owns one Qt object: a QWidget, or a QAction for menu items
  (GTK menu items are widgets, Qt's are actions - that mismatch is what made
  the old menus crash). A small bridge object turns Qt signals and events into
  GTK signals and GdkEvents, and Qt objects are deleted from an idle callback,
  so a widget can safely destroy itself from its own signal handler.

* Qt on Linux already runs on the GLib main loop, so g_timeout_add(),
  g_idle_add(), GIO async calls and GApplication simply work. GtkApplication is
  a real GApplication subclass.

* One library serves all three GTK generations. The API level is picked at
  compile time with -DGTKQT_API_VERSION=2|3|4, which the bundled drop-in
  pkg-config files (gtk+-2.0.pc, gtk+-3.0.pc, gtk4.pc) pass for you. Where the
  generations disagree (gtk_init() and gtk_window_new() lost their arguments
  in GTK 4; GTK 4 widgets are visible by default ...) the headers and runtime
  follow the generation the program was written for.

Dependencies: Qt 6 (or 5.15) Widgets, GLib/GObject/GIO/GModule, cairo, CMake.
GLib and cairo are plain C libraries, not GTK.


Building
--------

    cmake -S . -B build
    cmake --build build
    cd build && ctest            # headless: QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen

    cmake -S . -B build -DGTKQT_QT_MAJOR=5     # build against Qt 5.15 instead

Using it with an existing GTK project: point pkg-config at the shim and build
as usual.

    export PKG_CONFIG_PATH=/path/to/gtk-made-qt/build/pkgconfig
    gcc myapp.c $(pkg-config --cflags --libs gtk+-3.0)

"cmake --install" installs the same files to <prefix>/lib/gtk-made-qt/pkgconfig.

bgqt.sh builds and runs one example against the shim this way; bgtk.sh does
the same with the real GTK, for side-by-side comparison.

Debugging/test aid: GTKQT_AUTOQUIT=<ms> exits after that long and, with
GTKQT_SCREENSHOT=<file.png>, first saves every visible window to a PNG.


What is implemented
-------------------

Core          GtkWidget (show/hide/destroy, sensitivity, size requests,
              expand/align, tooltips, focus, accelerators, css classes),
              GtkContainer/GtkBin, GtkWindow, gtk_main/gtk_main_quit (nested),
              GdkEvent-based "*-event" signals, GTK 1/2 compatibility macros
              (gtk_signal_connect, GTK_OBJECT, gtk_timeout_add ...)
Layout        GtkBox (pack_start/pack_end with expand/fill/padding, and
              GTK 4 append), GtkHBox/VBox, GtkButtonBox, GtkGrid, GtkTable,
              GtkFixed, GtkFrame, GtkAlignment, GtkEventBox, GtkPaned,
              GtkNotebook, GtkStack + GtkStackSwitcher, GtkScrolledWindow,
              GtkViewport, GtkExpander, GtkHeaderBar, GtkSeparator, GtkStatusbar
Controls      GtkLabel (Pango markup subset, mnemonics, ellipsizing),
              GtkButton, GtkToggleButton, GtkCheckButton, GtkRadioButton
              (GSList groups and GTK 4 groups), GtkLinkButton, GtkSwitch,
              GtkEntry/GtkEditable/GtkEntryBuffer, GtkSpinButton, GtkComboBox,
              GtkComboBoxText, GtkCombo (GTK 1/2), GtkDropDown + GtkStringList,
              GtkImage (theme icons, stock ids, files), GtkSpinner,
              GtkAdjustment (public GTK 2 fields), GtkScale, GtkScrollbar,
              GtkProgressBar, GtkLevelBar, GtkColorButton
Text          GtkTextBuffer (a QTextDocument; several views per buffer),
              GtkTextIter, GtkTextMark, GtkTextTag, GtkTextView
Lists/trees   GtkListStore, GtkTreeStore (GValue rows, sorting, sort
              functions), GtkTreePath, GtkTreeView, GtkTreeViewColumn,
              GtkTreeSelection, text/toggle/pixbuf/progress cell renderers
              with attributes and cell data functions, "edited"/"toggled",
              GtkCellLayout combo boxes
Menus         GtkMenuBar, GtkMenu, GtkMenuItem, check/radio/separator/image
              items, GtkOptionMenu (GTK 1/2), popup menus, GtkToolbar and tool
              buttons, GtkAction/GtkActionGroup/GtkUIManager (GTK 2/3),
              GMenuModel menubars, GtkMenuButton, GtkPopover,
              GtkPopoverMenu(Bar)
Dialogs       GtkDialog (+ gtk_dialog_run, GTK 2 vbox/action_area fields),
              GtkMessageDialog, GtkAboutDialog (+ gtk_show_about_dialog),
              GtkFileChooserDialog, GtkFileChooserNative, GtkFileFilter, and the
              GTK 4.10 async GtkFileDialog / GtkAlertDialog
Application   GtkApplication, GtkApplicationWindow (a GActionMap: "win."
              actions), set_accels_for_action, menubar from GMenuModel,
              gtk_widget_insert_action_group, actionable buttons
Drawing       GtkDrawingArea with cairo: GTK 2 "expose-event" +
              gdk_cairo_create, GTK 3 "draw", GTK 4 draw functions;
              GTK 4 event controllers (click, drag, key, motion, scroll, focus)
GtkBuilder    GTK 2.12+/3/4 XML: properties, enums/flags, packing and layout
              child properties, internal children, GtkListStore data, <items>,
              <menu>, action widgets, signals (connect_signals in GTK 2/3,
              automatic in GTK 4)
GDK           events, key values (GDK_KEY_* and GTK 2 GDK_*), GdkRGBA/GdkColor
              parsing, minimal GdkPixbuf, screen size

The test suite (tests/test-api.c) runs the same C tests at all three API
levels; every example is also run as a smoke test.


What is missing (contributions welcome)
---------------------------------------

* CSS. Style providers are accepted and ignored; only a few style classes
  ("suggested-action", "destructive-action", "title", "dim-label",
  "monospace", "error") get a rough Qt equivalent. Apps look like Qt apps.
* GTK 4 list widgets (GtkListView, GtkColumnView, GtkGridView), GtkListBox,
  GtkFlowBox, GtkSizeGroup, GtkCalendar, GtkFontChooser, printing,
  drag-and-drop and the clipboard APIs.
* Pango as an API (pango_layout_* for text in cairo drawing) and most of
  GdkPixbuf.
* GTK 2 GDK drawing primitives (gdk_draw_*, GdkGC). gdk_cairo_create works
  inside expose handlers only.
* Text offsets are UTF-16 positions (only differs from GTK for characters
  outside the Basic Multilingual Plane).
* Widget margins only apply to containers; stack transitions are ignored.

Layout follows GTK's rules closely enough that windows come out at much the
same size - see docs/screenshots/range-widgets-gtk2.png (real GTK 2) next to
range-widgets-qt.png (this library).


History
-------

This repository started as two experiments from 2010-2014: "GTK made Qt"
(Johan Thelin; shim libraries) and "gqt" (Robin Burchell; one big header).
They were merged in 2014. Both mapped GTK types straight onto Qt classes
(typedef QWidget GtkWidget), which only worked when the GTK program was
compiled as C++.

The 2026 rewrite replaces that with a GObject-based C API so unmodified GTK
programs compile as C, moves the build to CMake and Qt 6, and extends coverage
from a handful of GTK 2 calls to large parts of GTK 2, 3 and 4. The original
GTK 2 examples in examples/gtk2 now build unchanged.
